  <article class="almanac-prose">
    <section id="one-tool" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>03.1 · one fixed schema</span>
        <h2>The model sees exactly one tool.</h2>
        <p>Whether a session has three capabilities or three hundred, the model-facing schema remains one function named <code>bash</code> with one string argument named <code>script</code>.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="one-tool-contract">
        <div><small>tool</small><strong><code>bash</code></strong></div>
        <div><small>input</small><strong><code>{ script: string }</code></strong></div>
        <div><small>result</small><strong>bounded output + exit code</strong></div>
      </div>

      <p>One script can discover capabilities, inspect schemas, loop over results, filter JSON, and make several calls. Adding a provider does not paste another large function definition into every model turn.</p>

      <div class="article-callout callout-decision">
        <strong>“bash” names a language the model recognizes.</strong>
        <p>It does not launch <code>/bin/bash</code>. <code>dekopon-shell</code> is a native Rust parser and interpreter with its own value model, builtins, limits, and one abstract capability seam.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="explore" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>03.2 · trained exploration</span>
        <h2>Reuse the bash training models already have.</h2>
        <p>Models know the rhythm of an unfamiliar host: list what exists, inspect one command, try a narrow operation, read the exit code, and adapt. Dekopon gives that learned behavior a smaller world to explore.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="shell-discovery">
        <div><span>$</span><code>cap --list</code><small>What commands exist?</small></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">↓</i>
        <div><span>$</span><code>cap --describe gh.pull-request.comment</code><small>What JSON does this one accept?</small></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">↓</i>
        <div><span>$</span><code>gh pr view 42 -R example/widgets</code><small>Read before writing.</small></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">↓</i>
        <div><span>$?</span><code>0, 1, 2, 126, or 127</code><small>Did it succeed, fail, get denied, or not exist?</small></div>
      </div>

      <p>The room is intentionally dark but not deceptive. Unsupported constructs fail by name. Ungranted capabilities are absent. Policy denial has a distinct exit code. The model can search for a valid path without discovering a hidden path to the host.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="commenter" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>03.3 · several operations, one tool call</span>
        <h2>A PR review becomes one replayable script.</h2>
        <p>The PR commenter can inspect the request, list changed files, build a concise summary, and leave a comment without another model round trip for every capability.</p>
      </div>

      <pre><code><span class="code-comment"># Model-authored Dekopon script</span>
pr=$(gh pr view 42 -R example/widgets)

if test "${pr[state]}" != "open"; then
  echo "PR 42 is not open; no comment left."
  exit 1
fi

count=$(gh pr files 42 -R example/widgets | jq '.files | length')

body=$(printf "Reviewed PR 42 at %s. It changes %s files." \
  "${pr[headSha]}" "$count")

gh pr review 42 -R example/widgets --comment --body "$body"</code></pre>

      <div class="script-legends">
        <span><i></i><strong>familiar</strong> variables, <code>if</code>, command substitution, pipes</span>
        <span><i></i><strong>structured</strong> JSON values remain values between commands</span>
        <span><i></i><strong>permissioned</strong> each <code>gh</code> subcommand maps to one capability</span>
      </div>

      <p>An operator can copy the same script into <code>dekopon-run shell</code> and replay it without a model. The <code>gh</code> vocabulary is ergonomic routing, not a bundled GitHub CLI: <code>gh api</code> is refused because raw passthrough would collapse the capability boundary.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="not-bash" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>03.4 · no operating-system shell</span>
        <h2>Familiar syntax. Radically smaller authority.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
        <table class="almanac-table strategy-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">The language keeps</th><th scope="col">The runtime deliberately lacks</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>if</code>, loops, functions, case, break, continue</td><td>process creation, job control, subshells, <code>exec</code>, <code>eval</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>variables, command substitution, arithmetic, here-documents</td><td>host environment variables, <code>export</code>, <code>source</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>jq</code>, grep, sed, cut, sort, base64, xargs</td><td>filesystem paths, descriptors, devices, real byte pipes</td></tr>
            <tr><td>capability-backed <code>gh</code> and optional <code>curl</code></td><td>raw sockets, DNS, ambient HTTP, a general API escape hatch</td></tr>
            <tr><td>optional UTC <code>date</code> after an explicit grant</td><td>clock access by default, timezone environment, setting time</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>The interpreter crate links no Wasmtime, broker, HTTP client, or filesystem code. Every external action reaches one <code>CapabilityInvoker</code>. The embedding process decides whether that capability is a pure local provider or a proposal to the separate broker.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="data" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>03.5 · JSON-native values</span>
        <h2>Structured data survives the whole plan.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="data-journey-map">
        <div class="journey-zone journey-zone-remote">
          <div class="journey-zone-label"><span>untrusted model output</span><strong>one script string</strong></div>
          <div class="journey-node"><small>tool argument</small><strong><code>{"script":"..."}</code></strong><code>strict object shape</code></div>
        </div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">↓ parse supported grammar</i>
        <div class="journey-zone journey-zone-host">
          <div class="journey-zone-label"><span><code>dekopon-shell</code></span><strong>JSON-native interpreter</strong></div>
          <div class="journey-node-row">
            <div class="journey-node"><small>variables</small><strong>arrays + objects</strong><code>not flattened to strings</code></div>
            <b aria-hidden="true">→</b>
            <div class="journey-node"><small>pipeline</small><strong>structured value</strong><code>jq-style handoff</code></div>
            <b aria-hidden="true">→</b>
            <div class="journey-node"><small>flags</small><strong>camelCase JSON</strong><code>--head-sha → headSha</code></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">↓ one capability proposal</i>
        <div class="journey-zone journey-zone-guest">
          <div class="journey-zone-label"><span>provider boundary</span><strong>strict typed operation</strong></div>
          <div class="journey-node"><small>result</small><strong>succeeded / failed / denied</strong><code>meaning preserved for the next command</code></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>Provider schemas help the model discover input, while providers still validate operation-specific values. A schema shown to an untrusted model is useful metadata; it is not an authorization mechanism.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="bounds" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>03.6 · a budget at every layer</span>
        <h2>Exploration is useful only when it ends.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="invariant-notes">
        <article><span>script</span><h3>Interpreter budget</h3><p>Steps, recursion, value bytes, output bytes and lines, deadline, and capability calls.</p></article>
        <article><span>session</span><h3>Model budget</h3><p>Model turns, tool calls per turn, and one capability-call budget shared across every script.</p></article>
        <article><span>Wasm</span><h3>Provider budget</h3><p>Fresh memory, fuel, input, output, and wall-clock bounds for each component call.</p></article>
        <article><span>broker</span><h3>Effect budget</h3><p>Policy, destination, method, HTTP calls, bytes, timeout, credential, and replay ID.</p></article>
      </div>

      <p>OpenTelemetry records each command word as a <code>shell.command</code> span with its known name, kind, argument count, exit code, and outcome. Argument values stay out because command arguments can be provider payloads wearing shell syntax.</p>

      {{ docsCta("Open the current shell language contract", "https://github.com/dekopon-agents/dekopon/blob/main/crates/dekopon-shell/README.md", "See the complete grammar, builtin behavior, rejected constructs, value semantics, residual limits, and observability fields.") }}
    </section>

